## The error

`logs/app.log:25107` (the only `ERROR` in 40k lines):

```
2026-09-24T01:57:30.145Z INFO  [billing] invoice 88121 start guest=1017 country=at total=4990
2026-09-24T01:57:30.145Z ERROR [billing] invoice 88121 failed: TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'rate')
    at taxFor (src/tax.js:13:30)
```

**Cause:** that invoice carried a lowercase country code, `country=at`. It's the only lowercase one in the entire log (vs. 3274 uppercase `AT`/`DE`/`FR`), and `src/tax.js` indexed its `RATES` table with the raw string — `RATES["at"]` is `undefined`, so `.rate` threw.

## The fix

**`src/tax.js`** — normalize the code to uppercase before lookup, and throw a descriptive error for a genuinely unknown country instead of an opaque `TypeError`:

```js
export function taxFor(cents, country) {
  const code = String(country).toUpperCase();
  const entry = RATES[code];
  if (!entry) {
    throw new Error(`no VAT rate configured for country "${country}"`);
  }
  return Math.round(cents * entry.rate);
}
```

**`tests/tax.test.js`** — regression tests: lowercase `at`/`de`/`fr` resolve to the right rates, and an unknown code (`zz`) throws a message naming the rejected code.

